Below is the JSON-like data you can parse (I’ve included a helpful note so you know how to interpret adjacency):

{
  'green_cells': [(1,3), (1,4), (2,2), (2,3), (3,2), (3,5), (3,6), (4,2), (4,6), (5,2), (5,6), (6,2), (6,6), (6,7), (7,2), (7,6), (7,7), (8,2), (8,3), (8,6), (8,7), (9,3), (9,6), (9,7)],
  'note': 'cells are row,col; adjacency includes diagonals for convenience',
}

Use this to proceed. If any cells look odd, you can ignore them or assume they connect diagonally.
